Task 1: Set Up Policies for Service Authentication
Subtask 1.1: Deploy Sample Applications
First, let's deploy a set of microservices that we'll use to demonstrate authentication policies.

Create a namespace for our lab:
kubectl create namespace secure-lab
kubectl label namespace secure-lab istio-injection=enabled
Deploy the sample applications:
Create a file called sample-apps.yaml:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: frontend
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: frontend
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: frontend
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: frontend
        image: nginx:1.20
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: frontend
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  selector:
    app: frontend
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: backend
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: backend
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: backend
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: backend
        image: httpd:2.4
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: backend
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  selector:
    app: backend
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: database
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: database
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: database
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: database
        image: postgres:13
        env:
        - name: POSTGRES_PASSWORD
          value: "password123"
        ports:
        - containerPort: 5432
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: database
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  selector:
    app: database
  ports:
  - port: 5432
    targetPort: 5432
Apply the configuration:
kubectl apply -f sample-apps.yaml
Verify the deployments:
kubectl get pods -n secure-lab
kubectl get services -n secure-lab
Subtask 1.2: Configure Mutual TLS Authentication
Now we'll implement mutual TLS (mTLS) to ensure all service-to-service communication is authenticated and encrypted.

Create a PeerAuthentication policy for strict mTLS:
Create a file called peer-authentication.yaml:

apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: default
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT
Apply the PeerAuthentication policy:
kubectl apply -f peer-authentication.yaml
Verify the policy is applied:
kubectl get peerauthentication -n secure-lab
Subtask 1.3: Test Authentication Without Proper Configuration
Let's test what happens when a service tries to communicate without proper mTLS configuration.

Deploy a test pod without Istio sidecar:
Create a file called test-pod.yaml:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: test-client
  namespace: secure-lab
  annotations:
    sidecar.istio.io/inject: "false"
spec:
  containers:
  - name: curl
    image: curlimages/curl:7.85.0
    command: ["/bin/sleep", "3600"]
Apply the test pod:
kubectl apply -f test-pod.yaml
Test connectivity from the non-mesh pod:
kubectl exec -n secure-lab test-client -- curl -s http://backend.secure-lab.svc.cluster.local
Expected Result: This should fail because the test pod doesn't have an Istio sidecar and cannot participate in mTLS communication.

Subtask 1.4: Test Authentication With Proper Configuration
Now let's test with a properly configured pod that has the Istio sidecar.

Deploy a test pod with Istio sidecar:
Create a file called mesh-test-pod.yaml:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: mesh-test-client
  namespace: secure-lab
  labels:
    app: test-client
spec:
  containers:
  - name: curl
    image: curlimages/curl:7.85.0
    command: ["/bin/sleep", "3600"]
Apply the mesh test pod:
kubectl apply -f mesh-test-pod.yaml
Test connectivity from the mesh-enabled pod:
kubectl exec -n secure-lab mesh-test-client -c curl -- curl -s http://backend.secure-lab.svc.cluster.local
Expected Result: This should succeed because both pods have Istio sidecars and can establish mTLS connections.

Task 2: Define Service-to-Service Communication Policies
Subtask 2.1: Create Basic Authorization Policies
Now we'll implement authorization policies to control which services can communicate with each other.

Create a default deny-all policy:
Create a file called deny-all-policy.yaml:

apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata:
  name: deny-all
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  {}
Apply the deny-all policy:
kubectl apply -f deny-all-policy.yaml
Test that all communication is now blocked:
kubectl exec -n secure-lab mesh-test-client -c curl -- curl -s http://backend.secure-lab.svc.cluster.local
Expected Result: This should fail with a 403 Forbidden error because we've denied all access by default.

Subtask 2.2: Allow Frontend to Backend Communication
Let's create a policy that allows the frontend service to communicate with the backend service.

Create an authorization policy for frontend-to-backend communication:
Create a file called frontend-to-backend-policy.yaml:

apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata:
  name: frontend-to-backend
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: backend
  rules:
  - from:
    - source:
        principals: ["cluster.local/ns/secure-lab/sa/default"]
        namespaces: ["secure-lab"]
    - source:
        principals: ["cluster.local/ns/secure-lab/sa/frontend"]
    to:
    - operation:
        methods: ["GET", "POST"]
    when:
    - key: source.labels[app]
      values: ["frontend"]
Apply the authorization policy:
kubectl apply -f frontend-to-backend-policy.yaml
Subtask 2.3: Allow Backend to Database Communication
Now let's create a policy for backend-to-database communication.

Create an authorization policy for backend-to-database communication:
Create a file called backend-to-database-policy.yaml:

apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata:
  name: backend-to-database
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: database
  rules:
  - from:
    - source:
        principals: ["cluster.local/ns/secure-lab/sa/default"]
        namespaces: ["secure-lab"]
    to:
    - operation:
        ports: ["5432"]
    when:
    - key: source.labels[app]
      values: ["backend"]
Apply the authorization policy:
kubectl apply -f backend-to-database-policy.yaml
Subtask 2.4: Create Role-Based Access Control
Let's implement more sophisticated authorization based on custom headers and service accounts.

Create a service account for the frontend:
kubectl create serviceaccount frontend-sa -n secure-lab
Update the frontend deployment to use the new service account:
Create a file called frontend-with-sa.yaml:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: frontend
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: frontend
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: frontend
        version: v1
        role: web-tier
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: frontend-sa
      containers:
      - name: frontend
        image: nginx:1.20
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
Apply the updated frontend deployment:
kubectl apply -f frontend-with-sa.yaml
Create an advanced authorization policy with role-based access:
Create a file called role-based-policy.yaml:

apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata:
  name: role-based-access
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: backend
  rules:
  - from:
    - source:
        principals: ["cluster.local/ns/secure-lab/sa/frontend-sa"]
    to:
    - operation:
        methods: ["GET"]
        paths: ["/api/public/*"]
  - from:
    - source:
        principals: ["cluster.local/ns/secure-lab/sa/frontend-sa"]
    to:
    - operation:
        methods: ["GET", "POST"]
        paths: ["/api/admin/*"]
    when:
    - key: request.headers[x-user-role]
      values: ["admin"]
Apply the role-based policy:
kubectl apply -f role-based-policy.yaml
Task 3: Test Policy Enforcement
Subtask 3.1: Test Allowed Communications
Let's verify that our policies are working correctly by testing allowed communications.

Create a test script to simulate frontend behavior:
Create a file called test-frontend.yaml:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: frontend-test
  namespace: secure-lab
  labels:
    app: frontend
spec:
  serviceAccountName: frontend-sa
  containers:
  - name: curl
    image: curlimages/curl:7.85.0
    command: ["/bin/sleep", "3600"]
Apply the frontend test pod:
kubectl apply -f test-frontend.yaml
Test allowed communication from frontend to backend:
kubectl exec -n secure-lab frontend-test -c curl -- curl -s -w "%{http_code}\n" http://backend.secure-lab.svc.cluster.local
Expected Result: Should return a 200 status code indicating successful communication.

Subtask 3.2: Test Blocked Communications
Now let's test that unauthorized communications are properly blocked.

Test communication from an unauthorized source:
kubectl exec -n secure-lab mesh-test-client -c curl -- curl -s -w "%{http_code}\n" http://backend.secure-lab.svc.cluster.local
Expected Result: Should return a 403 status code indicating access is forbidden.

Test direct database access from frontend (should be blocked):
kubectl exec -n secure-lab frontend-test -c curl -- curl -s -w "%{http_code}\n" http://database.secure-lab.svc.cluster.local:5432
Expected Result: Should fail or return an error because frontend shouldn't have direct database access.

Subtask 3.3: Test Header-Based Authorization
Let's test our role-based access control with custom headers.

Test access to admin endpoints without proper role:
kubectl exec -n secure-lab frontend-test -c curl -- curl -s -w "%{http_code}\n" http://backend.secure-lab.svc.cluster.local/api/admin/users
Expected Result: Should return 403 because no admin role header is provided.

Test access to admin endpoints with proper role header:
kubectl exec -n secure-lab frontend-test -c curl -- curl -s -w "%{http_code}\n" -H "x-user-role: admin" http://backend.secure-lab.svc.cluster.local/api/admin/users
Expected Result: Should return 200 or the backend's response because the admin role header is provided.

Subtask 3.4: Monitor and Debug Policy Enforcement
Let's learn how to monitor and debug our security policies.

Check the Istio proxy configuration:
istioctl proxy-config cluster mesh-test-client.secure-lab
View authorization policy status:
kubectl get authorizationpolicy -n secure-lab -o yaml
Check Envoy access logs for policy decisions:
kubectl logs -n secure-lab -l app=backend -c istio-proxy --tail=50
Use istioctl to analyze policy configuration:
istioctl analyze -n secure-lab
Subtask 3.5: Create Custom Deny Policies
Sometimes you need to explicitly deny certain types of access while allowing others.

Create a policy that denies DELETE operations:
Create a file called deny-delete-policy.yaml:

apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata:
  name: deny-delete
  namespace: secure-lab
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: backend
  rules:
  - to:
    - operation:
        methods: ["DELETE"]
    when:
    - key: source.labels[app]
      notValues: ["admin-service"]
  action: DENY
Apply the deny policy:
kubectl apply -f deny-delete-policy.yaml
Test that DELETE operations are blocked:
kubectl exec -n secure-lab frontend-test -c curl -- curl -s -w "%{http_code}\n" -X DELETE http://backend.secure-lab.svc.cluster.local/api/data
Expected Result: Should return 403 because DELETE operations are denied.

Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: mTLS Connection Failures
Symptoms: Services cannot communicate, receiving connection refused or SSL errors.

Solutions:

Verify that both services have Istio sidecars injected
Check that PeerAuthentication policy is correctly applied
Ensure namespace has istio-injection=enabled label
kubectl get pods -n secure-lab -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.containers[*].name}'
Issue 2: Authorization Policy Not Taking Effect
Symptoms: Policies are applied but access is still allowed/denied incorrectly.

Solutions:

Check policy syntax and selectors
Verify service account and label matching
Review policy precedence (DENY policies override ALLOW policies)
istioctl proxy-config rbac mesh-test-client.secure-lab -o json
Issue 3: Service Account Issues
Symptoms: Authorization based on service accounts not working.

Solutions:

Ensure service accounts exist and are assigned to pods
Verify the principal format: cluster.local/ns/NAMESPACE/sa/SERVICE_ACCOUNT
Check that pods are using the correct service account
kubectl get pods -n secure-lab -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.serviceAccountName}'
